// THE EXPLICIT TRAPPING SAMPLE -- the one place in samples/ where `in Trapping`
// is the feature under test, not a convenience. Everywhere else the corpus
// posture is: PROVE bounds (Exact, no annotation) or DECLARE wrap semantics
// (`in Wrapping`). Trapping means: this arithmetic is allowed to be partial,
// and overflowing it ABORTS the process at the faulting instruction.
//
// x = i32::MAX, then x + 1 -> the overflow check executes the target trap:
// ud2 on x86-64, brk #0 on AArch64. Unix reports SIGILL or SIGTRAP respectively;
// Windows x86-64 reports STATUS_ILLEGAL_INSTRUCTION (0xC000001D).
// The final exit_process(70) is NEVER reached. The companion canary
// (arithmetic/runtime_trapping_overflow_traps) requires the exact target trap,
// not merely a nonzero exit or another crash. See README.md for running this
// sample; it deliberately has no ordinary exit-code oracle.
// Warnings that this computation always overflows are expected.
use omega_language_std::console;
data Main { x: i32 in Trapping; }
machine Main::main(main: &mut Main) reaches Console {
    main.x = 2147483647;
    main.x = main.x + 1;
    // unreachable: the increment above traps.
    Console::exit_process(70);
}
